`@keyframes scan` used `translateY` percentages which resolve relative to the element's own height (`h-1` = 4px), giving the scan line only ~12px of travel inside a 160px circle — visually a no-op.

## Root cause

```css
/* Before: 100% of 4px = 4px → total travel: 12px */
@keyframes scan {
  0%   { transform: translateY(-100%); }
  100% { transform: translateY(200%); }
}
.animate-scan {
  animation: scan 2s ease-in-out infinite;
}
```

## Fix

Switch to `top`-based animation, which resolves against the **containing block** (the `overflow-hidden` circle at 160px), so the line sweeps the full height. `position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0` keeps it full-width without relying on document flow.

```css
/* After: top % resolves to container height → full 160px sweep */
@keyframes scan {
  0%   { top: -2px; }
  100% { top: 100%; }
}
.animate-scan {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  animation: scan 2s linear infinite;
}
```

`ease-in-out` → `linear` for a smooth looping sweep (no deceleration artifact at the loop boundary). No JSX changes needed; the existing `overflow-hidden rounded-full` container already clips both ends.

